/**
 * hx-boost sits on the whole shell, not just the browse pane, so that nav
 * links, the search form and the Filters form all swap the browse pane too
 * rather than reloading the document and destroying the map.
 *
 * hx-select pulls #content out of the full page Omeka returns; everything
 * else in the response -- including the map partial and its inline script --
 * is discarded, which is exactly why the map is never rebuilt. It does mean
 * the server renders the whole layout each time and we throw most of it away.
 * That is the cost of keeping Omeka's normal server-rendered routes rather
 * than inventing a fragment endpoint, and it is cheap next to re-running
 * every layer constructor and the full marker query on every click.
 *
 * Links carrying target/download (all the external CDASH links do) are
 * skipped by htmx and behave normally. With JS off, nothing is boosted and
 * every link is an ordinary full-page navigation -- which matters on a
 * public government site.
 */
?>
